import re

p4_prose = """<p>Implicit (non-declarative) memory operates without conscious, intentional recollection, manifesting instead as behavioral facilitation or performance modification resulting from prior experience. Unlike explicit memory, implicit memory operates under anoetic consciousness ("non-knowing") and is structurally independent of the medial temporal lobe (MTL) and hippocampus. Major implicit subtypes include procedural memory (motor skills and cognitive habits) and repetition priming (enhanced perceptual or conceptual processing of a stimulus following previous exposure).</p>
<p>Procedural memory supports the acquisition and execution of complex motor routines, such as mirror-tracing, pursuit rotor tracking, and instrument playing. Neurobiologically, procedural consolidation relies on corticostriatal loops linking the dorsal striatum (caudate and putamen), supplementary motor area (SMA), and cerebellum. In contrast, repetition priming is mediated by neocortical sensory and association areas. Perceptual priming manifests as more rapid or accurate identification of degraded sensory inputs (e.g., word-stem completion: recognizing "CHA___" after seeing "CHAIR") and is accompanied by neural repetition suppression—a reduction in fMRI BOLD signal in extrastriate visual cortex reflecting increased neural processing efficiency. Conceptual priming reflects semantic facilitation and recruits left inferior prefrontal cortex.</p>
<p>To dissociate procedural motor learning and repetition priming from explicit declarative memory, researchers tested three cohorts: Healthy Controls ($n = 20$), Amnesic Patients with bilateral hippocampal damage ($n = 12$, including etiology analogous to Patient H.M.), and Patients with Parkinson's Disease characterized by striatal dopamine depletion ($n = 15$). Participants completed: (1) a three-day Mirror-Tracing Task assessing motor procedural learning (quantified as percentage reduction in tracking errors between Day 1 and Day 3); (2) a Word-Stem Completion Priming Task (measuring percentage increase in producing previously studied words without explicit recall instructions); and (3) an Explicit Cued Recall Task (consciously remembering studied words). Visual extrastriate BOLD repetition suppression was simultaneously monitored via fMRI (Table 1).</p>"""

p_texts = re.findall(r"<p>(.*?)</p>", p4_prose, re.DOTALL)
prose = re.sub(r"<[^>]+>", " ", " ".join(p_texts))
words = prose.split()
print("Word count:", len(words))
